# Tables

- A page displaying a table must not scroll: the table fills the available
  space on its own, and long content is handled by pagination, not by page
  scroll.

- Only one table per interface: a single page or visible tab may display at
  most one table at a time.

- An empty table must occupy all the available space.

- Tables use a fixed table header.

- The table header can contain the filtering controls.

- Sorting must be indicated visually when a column is sortable.

- The table must use pagination when the number of rows is large.


- The table content can scroll horizontally or vertically inside its own
  dedicated area when necessary — this is the only scroll allowed around a
  table: a page with a table never scrolls.

## Responsive (table → cards)

- Under `md` (viewport width < 896px), `UserTable` automatically renders each row as a
  stacked card. Both views use the exact same columns, data, sorting and pagination.
- A card = one row: it MUST keep ALL the row's column values reachable inside the
  card (directly visible, or revealed by its in-place toggle): no dropped column,
  no horizontal scroll.
- The card mirrors the table look: its mechanics — card anatomy (which column
  becomes the title, the footer actions, the label/value rows), the
  `cardVisiblePairs` in-place "Voir tout" toggle and the opt-in `cardActions`
  enriched mode (per-card 3-dot menus, "Actions" toolbars above and below the
  card list) — are documented in `components/ui/user-table.tsx`. Do not
  duplicate them here.
- Card action menus follow `knowledge-base/components/dropdowns.mdc`: only one
  menu open at a time across the table, re-toggling closes, outside tap closes.

- Tables should have a clean, structured, information-dense appearance with clear
column hierarchy, readable rows, and subtle separation between content.

- When applicable, tables should support:
  - Column sorting.
  - Pagination.
  - Configurable number of rows per page.
  - Search and filtering.

